On Linux, we can see our LAN IP address through the command line via this command: <code>ifconfig</code><p>The output is cryptic and overwehelming but lets only focus on the last chunk since it gives us the information we care about the most.</p><code><pre>wlp3s0: flags=4163&lt;UP,BROADCAST,RUNNING,MULTICAST&gt;  mtu 1500
    inet 192.168.1.203  netmask 255.255.255.0  broadcast 192.168.1.255
    inet6 2600:1010:a121:9b5b:1aa1:9da1:8698:3a0  prefixlen 64  scopeid 0x0&lt;global&gt;
    inet6 2600:1010:a121:9b5b:3d70:e6fc:ffb1:8eab  prefixlen 64  scopeid 0x0&lt;global&gt;
    inet6 fe80::243a:9f1c:31c6:1048  prefixlen 64  scopeid 0x20&lt;link&gt;
    ether 50:e4:78:73:48:fd  txqueuelen 1000  (Ethernet)</pre></code><p><strong>Breakdown:</strong></p><ul><li><code>wlp3s0</code>: Network interface name (your Wi-Fi device).</li><li><code>flags=4163&lt;UP,BROADCAST,RUNNING,MULTICAST&gt;</code>: Interface is active and supports broadcasting and multicasting.</li><li><code>mtu 1500</code>: Maximum packet size the interface can handle (We will look at how packets work later on).</li><li><code>inet 192.168.1.203</code>: The holy grail of the command! This is your local IPv4 address (LAN IP).</li><li><code>netmask 255.255.255.0</code>: Subnet mask that divides network and host portions of the IP.</li><li><code>broadcast 192.168.1.255</code>: Address to send packets to all devices on the LAN.</li><li><code>inet6</code>: IPv6 addresses (A modern ip version address that we will look at later) assigned to your interface.</li><li><code>ether 50:e4:78:73:48:fd</code>: MAC (hardware) address of your network device.</li></ul><p>This command helps you see your device's IP configuration and network status.</p>
